You are working in the git repository checked out at /luxon (the Luxon date library, JS, jest tests). Task: fix a bug in /luxon/src/interval.js.

Bug report (from the user): There is a bug in `src/interval.js`. Once it is fixed, the test `Interval.fromISO works with Settings.throwOnInvalid` should pass; the version of it that checks this is not in the repository, so its name only describes the expected behaviour.

What is going on: `Interval.fromISO(text, opts)` (around line 134 of src/interval.js) calls `DateTime.fromISO(s, opts)` and `DateTime.fromISO(e, opts)`. When `Settings.throwOnInvalid === true`, `DateTime.fromISO` THROWS an InvalidDateTimeError instead of returning an invalid DateTime. So for inputs like `Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/P1Y")` (a start DateTime plus a Duration) or `Interval.fromISO("P1Y/2016-01-01")` (a Duration plus an end DateTime), the code throws instead of falling through to the Duration parsing branch. Expected behaviour: with `Settings.throwOnInvalid = true`, `Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/P1Y")`, `Interval.fromISO("P1Y/2016-01-01")` and `Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/2016-12-31")` all return valid Intervals, while a genuinely unparsable input such as `Interval.fromISO("blorp")` throws (because Interval.invalid throws when throwOnInvalid is set).

Required fix (implement exactly this shape, keeping the existing code style, prettier formatting and 2-space indentation): inside the `if (s && e)` block, wrap each DateTime.fromISO call in its own try/catch that records validity in a boolean, e.g.

    let start, startIsValid;
    try {
      start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
      startIsValid = start.isValid;
    } catch (e) {
      startIsValid = false;
    }

and the same for `end` / `endIsValid`, then branch on `startIsValid` / `endIsValid` instead of `start.isValid` / `end.isValid`. Do not change anything else in the file. Do not modify any file under /luxon/test (pre-existing tests must not be edited).

Verification you MUST run and report:
1. `cd /luxon && npx jest test/interval` — all interval tests must pass.
2. Add a TEMPORARY scratch test file OUTSIDE the repo test tree (e.g. /tmp/throwoninvalid.test.js is not enough because of module resolution; instead create /luxon/test/interval/tmp_throwoninvalid.test.js), run `cd /luxon && npx jest test/interval/tmp_throwoninvalid.test.js`, and then DELETE that temporary file so the repo is left clean (verify with `git status --porcelain`). The temporary test should set `Settings.throwOnInvalid = true` in a try/finally, then assert: `Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/P1Y").isValid === true`, `Interval.fromISO("P1Y/2016-01-01").isValid === true`, `Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/2016-12-31").isValid === true`, and `expect(() => Interval.fromISO("blorp")).toThrow()`. Import Settings and Interval from "../../src/luxon".
3. `cd /luxon && npx jest test/interval test/duration` — report results.

Report back: the exact diff you made (git diff src/interval.js), the exact commands run and their pass/fail summary lines, and confirmation that `git status --porcelain` shows only src/interval.js modified.

You are working in the git repository checked out at /luxon (the Luxon date library, JS, jest tests). Task: fix a bug in /luxon/src/impl/util.js.

Bug report (from the user): There is a bug in `src/impl/util.js`. Once it is fixed, the test `DateTime#plus works across the 100 barrier when passing through February` should pass; the version of it that checks this is not in the repository, so its name only describes the expected behaviour.

Context to investigate: `DateTime#plus` with a Duration containing months/years goes through `DateTime.plus` -> `adjustTime`/`Duration` math in src/datetime.js, and there is a helper in src/impl/util.js named `isUndefined`/`integerBetween`/`padStart`/`roundTo`/`isLeapYear`/`daysInMonth`/`daysInYear`/`weeksInWeekYear`/`isoWeekday`/`objToTS`/`normalizeObject`/`bestBy`/`clone`/`asNumber`/`isNumber`/`isInteger`/`isString`/`isDate`/`numberBetween`/`floorMod`/`isLeapYear`/`daysInMonth`/`daysInYear`/`weeksInWeekYear`/`untruncateYear`/`parseInteger`/`signedOffset`/`signedOffsetFromString`/`hasOwnProperty`/`validateWeekSettings`/`validateStartEnd` etc. Read the whole file and figure out which helper is wrong.

Strong hint: the failing scenario is adding months to a DateTime such that the resulting year crosses the year-100 barrier (e.g. `DateTime.fromObject({ year: 100, month: 1, day: 1 }).plus({ months: 1 })` style cases, or years around 99/100/101) while passing through February. The likely culprit is the year-untruncation helper `untruncateYear` (or a similar two-digit-year expansion helper) which uses a threshold like `year > 60 ? 1900 + year : 2000 + year` and is being applied where it should not be (e.g. inside `DateTime.plus`'s month/year adjustment or inside `objToTS`/`normalizeObject`). Investigate by reading src/datetime.js (look at `plus`, `adjustTime`, `set`, `objToTS`, `normalizeObject`) and src/impl/util.js, then reproduce the bug with a small script run through jest or babel-node.

Reproduction guidance: create a TEMPORARY test file at /luxon/test/datetime/tmp_barrier.test.js (and DELETE it when done) that does something like:

    import { DateTime } from "../../src/luxon";
    test("DateTime#plus works across the 100 barrier when passing through February", () => {
      const dt = DateTime.fromObject({ year: 100, month: 1, day: 1 });
      const result = dt.plus({ months: 1 });
      expect(result.year).toBe(100);
      expect(result.month).toBe(2);
    });

Run it with `cd /luxon && npx jest test/datetime/tmp_barrier.test.js` and iterate until you understand the real failure. Then find the minimal, correct fix in src/impl/util.js (or, if the root cause is genuinely in another src file, fix it there — but the user says the bug is in src/impl/util.js, so prefer that).

Constraints:
- Do NOT modify or delete anything under /luxon/test (pre-existing tests must not be edited). The temporary test file you create must be deleted before you finish.
- Do not implement new features; only fix the bug.
- Keep prettier formatting (run `npx prettier --list-different "src/**/*.js"`).

Verification you MUST run and report:
1. `cd /luxon && npx jest test/datetime` — all must pass (report summary lines).
2. `cd /luxon && npx jest` (full suite) — report summary lines and any failing test names.
3. `cd /luxon && npx eslint --quiet src test benchmarks` — report errors.
4. `cd /luxon && git status --porcelain` — must show only the intended src file(s) modified, no test files.

Report back: the root cause, the exact diff (git diff), the commands run with their pass/fail summary lines, and confirmation the temp test file was deleted.

You are working in the git repository checked out at /luxon (the Luxon date library, JS, jest tests). Task: fix a bug in /luxon/src/impl/util.js.

Bug report (from the user): There is a bug in `src/impl/util.js`. Once it is fixed, the test `DateTime#toISODate() correctly pads negative years` should pass; the version of it that checks this is not in the repository, so its name only describes the expected behaviour.

Investigate: `DateTime#toISODate()` (in /luxon/src/datetime.js) formats the date with a technical format string; negative (BCE) years must be padded correctly, e.g. year -1 should render as `-0001-01-01` and year -100 as `-0100-01-01`, not `-1-01-01` or `00-1-01-01`. The likely culprit is the helper `padStart(input, n = 2)` in /luxon/src/impl/util.js:

    export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
      if (input.toString().length < n) {
        return ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);
      } else {
        return input.toString();
      }
    }

For a negative input like -1 with n=4 this produces `00-1` instead of `-001`. Read /luxon/src/datetime.js's `toISODate`, `toISO`, `toISOWeekDate`, and /luxon/src/impl/formatter.js's handling of the `yyyy`/`yyyyy`/`yyyyyy` tokens to see exactly how the year string is built, then fix the padding so negative years are padded to the requested width including the sign.

Steps:
1. Read the relevant code and reproduce with a TEMPORARY test file /luxon/test/datetime/tmp_negpad.test.js containing e.g.:

    import { DateTime } from "../../src/luxon";
    test("DateTime#toISODate() correctly pads negative years", () => {
      expect(DateTime.fromObject({ year: -1, month: 1, day: 1 }, { zone: "utc" }).toISODate()).toBe("-0001-01-01");
      expect(DateTime.fromObject({ year: -100, month: 1, day: 1 }, { zone: "utc" }).toISODate()).toBe("-0100-01-01");
      expect(DateTime.fromObject({ year: -10, month: 1, day: 1 }, { zone: "utc" }).toISODate()).toBe("-0010-01-01");
    });

   Run `cd /luxon && npx jest test/datetime/tmp_negpad.test.js` and iterate until you understand the real failure. Be careful to determine the intended width convention from the existing tests in /luxon/test/datetime (read them, do not edit them) and from the docstrings — do not guess wildly. If the existing repo tests indicate a different expected width for negative years, follow the repo's own evidence and explain your reasoning in the report.
2. Implement the minimal correct fix in /luxon/src/impl/util.js (only that file). Do not implement new features. Keep the existing code style and prettier formatting.

Constraints:
- Do NOT modify or delete anything under /luxon/test (pre-existing tests must not be edited). Your temporary test file must be DELETED before you finish.
- IMPORTANT: other subagents may be concurrently editing /luxon/src/datetime.js and /luxon/src/duration.js — do not touch those files, and do not touch /luxon/src/interval.js. Note that /luxon/src/impl/util.js already contains a fix to `objToLocalTS` (a comment about leap years) — keep that fix intact.
- Ignore any leftover temp files like /luxon/test/datetime/tmp_isdst.test.js or /luxon/test/duration/tmp_normalize.test.js.

Verification you MUST run and report:
1. `cd /luxon && npx jest test/datetime 2>&1 | tail -12` — report summary lines and failing test names (note: 2 pre-existing failures in test/datetime/toFormat.test.js about French hour padding, and possibly a foreign temp test about isInDST, are expected and unrelated).
2. `cd /luxon && npx jest 2>&1 | tail -12` (full suite) — report summary lines and failing test names.
3. `cd /luxon && npx eslint --quiet src` — report errors.
4. `cd /luxon && npx prettier --list-different "src/impl/util.js"` — report output.
5. `cd /luxon && git status --porcelain` — report what you see.

Report back: the root cause, the exact diff (git diff src/impl/util.js), the commands run with their pass/fail summary lines, and confirmation your temp test file was deleted.

You are working in the git repository checked out at /luxon (the Luxon date library, JS, jest tests). Task: fix a bug in /luxon/src/zones/fixedOffsetZone.js.

Bug report (from the user): There is a bug in `src/zones/fixedOffsetZone.js`. Once it is fixed, the tests `A DateTime in an invalid FixedOffsetZone is itself invalid` and `FixedOffsetZone is invalid when constructed with a non-numeric offset` should pass; the versions of them that check this are not in the repository, so their names only describe the expected behaviour.

Investigate: read /luxon/src/zones/fixedOffsetZone.js and /luxon/src/zones/zone.js (the base class, which defines `isValid`), plus /luxon/src/impl/zoneUtil.js (`normalizeZone`) and how DateTime handles an invalid zone (see `unsupportedZone` and `DateTime.fromObject`/`setZone` in /luxon/src/datetime.js). Expected behaviour: `FixedOffsetZone.instance("blorp")` (or `FixedOffsetZone.instance(NaN)`, or any non-numeric offset) must produce an INVALID zone (`isValid === false`, `offset` probably NaN), and a DateTime created with such a zone (e.g. `DateTime.fromObject({year: 2020, month: 1, day: 1}, {zone: FixedOffsetZone.instance("blorp")})`, or `DateTime.now().setZone(...)`) must itself be invalid (`isValid === false`). Currently the constructor probably coerces with `Number(offset)`/parseInt and never validates, so a bad offset silently becomes a valid zone.

Steps:
1. Read the code and reproduce with a TEMPORARY test file /luxon/test/zones/tmp_fixedoffset.test.js containing something like:

    import { DateTime, FixedOffsetZone } from "../../src/luxon";
    test("FixedOffsetZone is invalid when constructed with a non-numeric offset", () => {
      expect(FixedOffsetZone.instance("blorp").isValid).toBe(false);
    });
    test("A DateTime in an invalid FixedOffsetZone is itself invalid", () => {
      const dt = DateTime.fromObject({ year: 2020, month: 1, day: 1 }, { zone: FixedOffsetZone.instance("blorp") });
      expect(dt.isValid).toBe(false);
    });

   (Check how FixedOffsetZone is exported from src/luxon.js first; adjust the import if needed.) Run `cd /luxon && npx jest test/zones/tmp_fixedoffset.test.js` and iterate until you understand the real failure. Also read the existing tests in /luxon/test/zones/ (do not edit them) to learn the intended contract, and make sure your fix keeps them passing.
2. Implement the minimal correct fix in /luxon/src/zones/fixedOffsetZone.js (only that file, unless the fix genuinely requires a tiny change elsewhere — if so, explain why). Do not implement new features. Keep the existing code style and prettier formatting.

Constraints:
- Do NOT modify or delete anything under /luxon/test (pre-existing tests must not be edited). Your temporary test file must be DELETED before you finish.
- IMPORTANT: other subagents may be concurrently editing /luxon/src/datetime.js and /luxon/src/duration.js — do not touch those files, and do not touch /luxon/src/interval.js or /luxon/src/impl/util.js. Ignore any leftover temp files like /luxon/test/datetime/tmp_isdst.test.js.

Verification you MUST run and report:
1. `cd /luxon && npx jest test/zones 2>&1 | tail -12` — report summary lines.
2. `cd /luxon && npx jest 2>&1 | tail -12` (full suite) — report summary lines and failing test names (note: 2 pre-existing failures in test/datetime/toFormat.test.js about French hour padding, plus possibly a foreign temp isInDST test, are expected and unrelated).
3. `cd /luxon && npx eslint --quiet src` — report errors.
4. `cd /luxon && npx prettier --list-different "src/zones/fixedOffsetZone.js"` — report output.
5. `cd /luxon && git status --porcelain` — report what you see.

Report back: the root cause, the exact diff (git diff src/zones/fixedOffsetZone.js), the commands run with their pass/fail summary lines, and confirmation your temp test file was deleted.

You are working in the git repository checked out at /luxon (the Luxon date library, JS, jest tests). Task: fix a bug in /luxon/src/datetime.js.

Bug report (from the user): There is a bug in `src/datetime.js`. Once it is fixed, the test `DateTime#isInDST() returns true for 1974 whole year in USA- from January 6th to October 27th` should pass; the version of it that checks this is not in the repository, so its name only describes the expected behaviour.

Background: In 1974 the USA observed year-round daylight saving time (the Emergency Daylight Saving Time Energy Conservation Act). DST began on 1974-01-06 and effectively ran until 1974-10-27, so a DateTime in the America/New_York zone for e.g. 1974-06-01 should report `isInDST() === true`, and dates before Jan 6 1974 (e.g. 1974-01-01) or after Oct 27 1974 should report false. The implementation of `isInDST()` lives in /luxon/src/datetime.js (search for `isInDST`); it likely compares the DateTime's offset with the offsets of a couple of probe dates (e.g. Jan 1 and Jun 1 of the same year) and can get the wrong answer for years where the DST rules are unusual. Read the current implementation carefully and work out the correct fix.

Note: the expected behaviour must be derivable from the repository itself (docstrings/tests) — do not rely on outside knowledge of luxon's git history. The docstring for `isInDST` in src/datetime.js is the contract; read it and the surrounding code.

Steps:
1. Read the `isInDST` implementation in /luxon/src/datetime.js and any related helpers.
2. Reproduce: create a TEMPORARY test file /luxon/test/datetime/tmp_isdst.test.js containing something like:

    import { DateTime } from "../../src/luxon";
    test("DateTime#isInDST() returns true for 1974 whole year in USA- from January 6th to October 27th", () => {
      const zone = "America/New_York";
      expect(DateTime.fromObject({ year: 1974, month: 6, day: 1 }, { zone }).isInDST()).toBe(true);
      expect(DateTime.fromObject({ year: 1974, month: 1, day: 6 }, { zone }).isInDST()).toBe(true);
      expect(DateTime.fromObject({ year: 1974, month: 10, day: 26 }, { zone }).isInDST()).toBe(true);
      expect(DateTime.fromObject({ year: 1974, month: 1, day: 1 }, { zone }).isInDST()).toBe(false);
      expect(DateTime.fromObject({ year: 1974, month: 11, day: 1 }, { zone }).isInDST()).toBe(false);
    });

   Run `cd /luxon && npx jest test/datetime/tmp_isdst.test.js` and iterate until you understand the real failure. Note the container's ICU tz database may or may not contain the 1974 rule; if the platform's zone data disagrees, still make the implementation correct/robust per the docstring contract, and report exactly what you observed.
3. Implement the minimal correct fix in /luxon/src/datetime.js (only that file). Do not implement new features. Keep the existing code style and prettier formatting.

Constraints:
- Do NOT modify or delete anything under /luxon/test (pre-existing tests must not be edited). Your temporary test file must be DELETED before you finish.
- IMPORTANT: another subagent may be concurrently editing /luxon/src/impl/util.js — do not touch that file. Also do not touch /luxon/src/interval.js.
- If you see an unrelated temp file /luxon/test/datetime/tmp_barrier.test.js, leave it alone.

