You are working in the git repository checked out at /luxon (the Luxon date library, JS, jest tests). Task: fix a bug in /luxon/src/duration.js.

Bug report (from the user): There is a bug in `src/duration.js`. Once it is fixed, the test `Duration#normalize can convert all unit pairs` should pass; the version of it that checks this is not in the repository, so its name only describes the expected behaviour.

Background: `Duration#normalize()` is implemented in /luxon/src/duration.js. It is supposed to normalize a duration so that all its units are expressed in terms of the largest unit present (e.g. `Duration.fromObject({ minutes: 60 }).normalize().toObject()` -> `{ hours: 1 }`; `Duration.fromObject({ hours: 1, minutes: 60 }).normalize()` -> `{ hours: 2 }`). The test name says it must work for ALL unit pairs (milliseconds, seconds, minutes, hours, days, weeks, months, quarters, years, and combinations like seconds->minutes, minutes->hours, hours->days, days->weeks, weeks->months, months->years, etc.). Read the implementation of `normalize`, `shiftTo`, `as`, `toObject`, and the conversion matrices (casual / longterm) in src/duration.js and find the defect.

Steps:
1. Read `normalize()` and related code in /luxon/src/duration.js carefully.
2. Reproduce with a TEMPORARY test file /luxon/test/duration/tmp_normalize.test.js that loops over all unit pairs, e.g.:

    import { Duration } from "../../src/luxon";
    const units = ["years", "quarters", "months", "weeks", "days", "hours", "minutes", "seconds", "milliseconds"];
    test("Duration#normalize can convert all unit pairs", () => {
      for (const big of units) {
        for (const small of units) {
          const d = Duration.fromObject({ [big]: 1, [small]: 1 });
          const n = d.normalize();
          expect(n.isValid).toBe(true);
          // the normalized duration must equal the original when converted to a common unit
          expect(n.as("milliseconds")).toBeCloseTo(d.as("milliseconds"), 3);
        }
      }
    });

   Run `cd /luxon && npx jest test/duration/tmp_normalize.test.js` and iterate until you understand the real failure. Note: some pairs may legitimately be asymmetric (e.g. normalizing when the smaller unit is bigger); reason from the docstring of `normalize` in src/duration.js and from the existing tests in /luxon/test/duration/normalize.test.js (read them, do not edit them) to determine the intended contract.
3. Implement the minimal correct fix in /luxon/src/duration.js (only that file). Do not implement new features. Keep the existing code style and prettier formatting.

Constraints:
- Do NOT modify or delete anything under /luxon/test (pre-existing tests must not be edited). Your temporary test file must be DELETED before you finish.
- IMPORTANT: other subagents may be concurrently editing /luxon/src/datetime.js and /luxon/src/impl/util.js — do not touch those files, and do not touch /luxon/src/interval.js. Ignore any leftover temp files like /luxon/test/datetime/tmp_barrier.test.js or /luxon/test/datetime/tmp_isdst.test.js.

